window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- HISTORY ---------- */
{id:"his_renaissance",cat:"his",t:"Renaissance",
 s:"The period in Europe, roughly from the 14th to the 16th century, when art and learning blossomed again.",
 l:"The Renaissance began in the cities of Italy, especially Florence, and spread across Europe. Scholars rediscovered ancient Greek and Roman texts and put people, their talents and their curiosity at the centre of their thinking – an approach called humanism. Painters studied perspective and the human body, architects copied classical columns and domes, and the printing press spread new ideas quickly. It was not a sudden break with the Middle Ages but a gradual change of interests and style.",
 x:"Leonardo da Vinci, painter, engineer and inventor, is often called the perfect Renaissance man.",
 f:"Renaissance is French for “rebirth”. The Italian artist and writer Giorgio Vasari already spoke of a “rinascita” of the arts in his book of artists' lives, published in 1550.",
 js:"A time long ago when art and science became exciting again.",
 jl:"About six hundred years ago, people in Italy started reading old books from ancient Greece and Rome again. Artists learned to paint faces and bodies that look real, and builders made beautiful domes. This time of new ideas is called the Renaissance, which means “rebirth”.",
 jx:"In the museum, Zoe saw a Renaissance painting where the faces looked almost like photos."},
{id:"his_feudalism",cat:"his",t:"Feudalism",
 s:"The medieval system in which land was given in exchange for loyalty and service.",
 l:"Feudalism is a name for the way much of medieval Europe was organised. A king granted land to powerful lords; in return they promised loyalty and sent knights to fight for him. The lords gave smaller pieces of land to lesser knights, and peasants worked the fields, many of them as serfs who could not leave their lord's land. Historians today debate how useful the word is, because the arrangements differed a lot from country to country and century to century.",
 x:"Under feudalism, a peasant's life depended largely on the lord who owned the village.",
 f:"Nobody in the Middle Ages called their world “feudal”. The term was invented by scholars and lawyers centuries later, from the medieval Latin feodum – a piece of land held in return for service.",
 js:"An old system where land was given in return for help and loyalty.",
 jl:"Long ago, a king gave land to lords. The lords promised to help the king, for example by sending knights. Farmers worked on the lords' land and had to give them part of their harvest. This way of living is called feudalism.",
 jx:"In our history lesson we drew a pyramid of feudalism: the king at the top and the farmers at the bottom."},
{id:"his_primary_source",cat:"his",t:"Primary source",
 s:"Evidence created at the time being studied, such as a letter, photo or official record.",
 l:"A primary source is a first-hand trace of the past: a diary, a law, a newspaper from that day, a map, a coin, a recording or an object someone used. A secondary source is a later work that interprets primary sources, like a textbook or a history book. Historians always ask who made a source, when, why and for whom, because even first-hand evidence can be one-sided, mistaken or made to persuade. Comparing several sources is the best way to get close to what really happened.",
 x:"For her project, Ella used her great-grandfather's letters from 1945 as a primary source.",
 f:"Even ancient scribbles count. In Egypt, workers' notes written on broken pottery – called ostraca – tell historians about sick days, shopping and quarrels more than three thousand years ago.",
 js:"Something made at the time in the past, like an old letter or photo.",
 jl:"If you want to learn about the past, the best clues are things made back then: old letters, photos, tickets or toys. These are primary sources. A book written later about that time is a secondary source.",
 jx:"Grandma's school report from 1970 is a primary source about her school days."},
{id:"his_anachronism",cat:"his",t:"Anachronism",
 s:"Something placed in the wrong period of time.",
 l:"An anachronism is a person, object, word or idea that appears in a time where it does not belong – like a wristwatch in a film about ancient Rome or a knight talking about “uploading” something. Anachronisms are often mistakes, but writers also use them on purpose for humour or to make an old story feel close to today. The word can also describe something old-fashioned that survives in the present, such as a custom that no longer has a clear purpose.",
 x:"The film about the Vikings was fun, but the zip on the hero's jacket was an obvious anachronism.",
 f:"In Shakespeare's play Julius Caesar a clock strikes three – although striking mechanical clocks did not exist in ancient Rome. The word comes from Greek ana, “backwards”, and chronos, “time”.",
 js:"Something that is in the wrong time, like a phone in a castle.",
 jl:"Imagine a picture of dinosaurs with a car in the background. That's silly, because cars came millions of years later! A thing that doesn't fit the time is called an anachronism.",
 jx:"In my comic the pharaoh is holding a mobile phone – a funny anachronism."},
{id:"his_bce",cat:"his",t:"BC and AD (BCE and CE)",
 s:"Labels that count years before and after the traditional date of Jesus's birth.",
 l:"In the system used in most of the world, years are counted from the traditional year of Jesus's birth. Years before it are marked BC (“before Christ”) and counted backwards, so 500 BC is earlier than 400 BC. Years after it are AD, from Latin anno Domini, “in the year of the Lord”. Many scholars use the neutral labels BCE (“before the Common Era”) and CE (“Common Era”), which keep the same numbers but leave out the religious meaning.",
 x:"The Great Pyramid of Giza was built around 2560 BC, long before the Romans arrived in Britain in AD 43.",
 f:"There is no year zero: 1 BC is followed directly by AD 1. The system was worked out by a monk called Dionysius Exiguus in the 6th century, and most historians think he missed the real date of Jesus's birth by a few years.",
 js:"A way of counting years: BC is before year one, AD is after.",
 jl:"We count years from a starting point. Years after it are AD, like this year. Years before it are BC, and they count backwards – so the bigger the number, the longer ago. Some books write BCE and CE instead; they mean the same years.",
 jx:"The Romans built Hadrian's Wall in the 120s AD, but Stonehenge is from many centuries BC."},
{id:"his_middle_ages",cat:"his",t:"Middle Ages",
 s:"The period of European history between ancient times and the modern era, roughly 500 to 1500.",
 l:"The Middle Ages, or medieval period, is usually dated from the fall of the Western Roman Empire in 476 to the late 15th century. It saw castles and cathedrals, knights and monasteries, the growth of towns and the first universities, such as Bologna, Paris and Oxford. People once called it the “Dark Ages”, but historians now stress its inventions, trade and art. Medieval people did not see themselves as living “in the middle” – that label came later.",
 x:"The town still has its medieval walls, built in the Middle Ages to protect merchants and craftsmen.",
 f:"The name comes from Renaissance scholars, who saw the centuries between ancient Rome and their own time as a gap – a medium aevum, Latin for “middle age”.",
 js:"The time of castles, knights and monks, about a thousand years ago.",
 jl:"The Middle Ages lasted about a thousand years. People built castles and huge churches, knights rode horses, and monks copied books by hand. Most people were farmers. Towns grew, and the first universities opened.",
 jx:"On our school trip we visited a castle from the Middle Ages and climbed the tower."},
{id:"his_industrial_revolution",cat:"his",t:"Industrial Revolution",
 s:"The change from making things by hand to making them with machines in factories.",
 l:"The Industrial Revolution began in Britain in the second half of the 18th century and spread to Europe, North America and beyond. Steam engines, powered by coal, drove machines in textile mills, pumped water out of mines and later pulled trains. Goods became cheaper and cities grew fast, as people moved from the countryside to work in factories. It also brought long working hours, child labour and polluted, crowded towns, which led to new laws and the first trade unions.",
 x:"During the Industrial Revolution, a small market town could become a factory city in a few decades.",
 f:"The world's first public railway to use steam locomotives, the Stockton and Darlington Railway in north-east England, opened in 1825.",
 js:"When people started using big machines and factories to make things.",
 jl:"Long ago, almost everything was made by hand at home. Then people invented machines that worked with steam, and built factories and railways. Things could be made much faster. This big change is called the Industrial Revolution.",
 jx:"The old factory by the river, now a museum, was built during the Industrial Revolution."},
{id:"his_enlightenment",cat:"his",t:"Enlightenment",
 s:"An 18th-century movement that put reason, science and human rights first.",
 l:"The Enlightenment, or Age of Reason, was a movement of thinkers in 18th-century Europe who believed that people should use their own reason rather than simply accept tradition or authority. Writers such as Voltaire, Montesquieu, Locke and Kant argued for freedom of thought, religious tolerance, the separation of powers and education for all. Their ideas inspired the American and French revolutions and many modern constitutions. Critics note that its ideals were not applied equally to everyone at the time.",
 x:"The idea that a government needs the consent of the people is a typical Enlightenment idea.",
 f:"In 1784 the philosopher Immanuel Kant summed up the Enlightenment with the Latin motto “Sapere aude” – “Dare to know” or “Have the courage to use your own understanding”.",
 js:"A time when people said: think for yourself and use science.",
 jl:"About three hundred years ago, some writers said: don't just believe what you are told – think, ask questions and check things with experiments. They also said every person has rights. This time of new ideas is called the Enlightenment, like switching on a light.",
 jx:"Our teacher said asking “How do we know that?” is very much in the spirit of the Enlightenment."},
{id:"his_reformation",cat:"his",t:"Reformation",
 s:"The 16th-century religious movement that split Western Christianity and created Protestant churches.",
 l:"The Reformation started in 1517, when the German monk Martin Luther published his 95 Theses criticising practices of the Catholic Church, especially the sale of indulgences. He taught that faith and the Bible mattered more than church rules. Other reformers, such as John Calvin, followed, and many regions broke away from Rome, forming Lutheran, Reformed and Anglican churches. The printing press spread these ideas fast. The split led to long conflicts, but also to new schools and Bibles in everyday languages.",
 x:"Thanks to the Reformation, many people could read the Bible in their own language for the first time.",
 f:"The famous image of Luther nailing his theses to a church door in Wittenberg may be a legend – historians still debate whether it really happened that way.",
 js:"A time when Christians in Europe split into different churches.",
 jl:"About five hundred years ago, a monk called Martin Luther said the church was doing some things wrong. Many people agreed with him, and new churches were started. This is called the Reformation. Luther also translated the Bible into German so ordinary people could read it.",
 jx:"Our guide said this church became Protestant during the Reformation."},
{id:"his_silk_road",cat:"his",t:"Silk Road",
 s:"Ancient trade routes linking China with Central Asia, the Middle East and Europe.",
 l:"The Silk Road was not a single road but a network of land and sea routes used for well over a thousand years. Merchants carried silk, spices, paper, porcelain, horses and glass, usually passing goods from one trader to the next rather than travelling the whole way. Along with goods travelled religions, inventions, languages and, sadly, diseases. Oasis cities such as Samarkand grew rich from this trade.",
 x:"Paper-making reached the Middle East and later Europe along the Silk Road.",
 f:"The name is fairly modern: the German geographer Ferdinand von Richthofen used the term “Seidenstraße”, Silk Road, in 1877.",
 js:"Old trade paths between China and Europe.",
 jl:"Long ago, traders with camels and ships carried silk, spices and paper between China and faraway lands. They used many paths, together called the Silk Road. Stories, games and ideas travelled along it too.",
 jx:"Marco Polo's stories describe cities along the Silk Road."},
{id:"his_cold_war",cat:"his",t:"Cold War",
 s:"The long rivalry between the United States and the Soviet Union after the Second World War.",
 l:"The Cold War lasted roughly from the late 1940s to 1991. The United States and its allies faced the Soviet Union and the countries it controlled in Eastern Europe. It was called “cold” because the two superpowers never fought each other directly; instead they competed through an arms race, spying, propaganda, the space race and wars fought elsewhere. Europe was divided by the Iron Curtain, and the Berlin Wall became its symbol. It ended with the fall of communism in Eastern Europe and the break-up of the Soviet Union.",
 x:"During the Cold War, the race to the Moon was also a race for prestige.",
 f:"George Orwell, author of Nineteen Eighty-Four, used the phrase “cold war” in a 1945 essay, before it became the common name for the conflict.",
 js:"A long quarrel between two big countries that didn't turn into a real war between them.",
 jl:"After a big war, two powerful countries, the USA and the Soviet Union, didn't trust each other for about forty years. They competed to build rockets, send people into space and win friends around the world. It was called the Cold War because they never fought each other directly.",
 jx:"Grandpa remembers the Cold War, when Germany was split into two countries."},
{id:"his_colonialism",cat:"his",t:"Colonialism",
 s:"When a country takes control of other lands and peoples and rules them for its own benefit.",
 l:"Colonialism means one state taking over territory abroad, settling or governing it and using its land, resources and people. From the 15th to the 20th century, European powers such as Spain, Portugal, Britain, France and the Netherlands built colonies in the Americas, Africa, Asia and Oceania. Local people often lost land, freedom and lives, and borders were drawn without them. After the Second World War most colonies became independent – a process called decolonisation – but its effects are still felt today.",
 x:"Many borders in Africa were drawn by European powers during colonialism, not by the people living there.",
 f:"In 1960 alone, seventeen African countries gained independence, so that year is often called the “Year of Africa”.",
 js:"When a country takes over another land far away and rules it.",
 jl:"Long ago, some powerful countries sailed far away and took control of other lands, even though people already lived there. They ruled them and took their riches. This is called colonialism. Later, those lands fought for and won their freedom.",
 jx:"Our teacher explained that India was ruled by Britain until 1947, during colonialism."},
{id:"his_chronology",cat:"his",t:"Chronology",
 s:"The order in which events happened, or the science of dating them.",
 l:"Chronology means arranging events in the order they happened and working out their dates. A timeline is a simple chronology. Historians use written records, lists of rulers, astronomical events such as eclipses, and scientific methods like radiocarbon dating to fix dates. Getting the order right matters because it shows what could have caused what: an event cannot be caused by something that happened later.",
 x:"To understand the war, we first wrote down the chronology of the main battles.",
 f:"Tree rings can date wood to the exact year. This method, dendrochronology, can show when the trees used to build an old house were cut down.",
 js:"Putting things in the order they happened.",
 jl:"Chronology means putting events in the right order: first this, then that. When you make a timeline of your life – born, first steps, first day at school – you are making a chronology!",
 jx:"Mia made a chronology of the dinosaurs, from the oldest to the youngest."},
{id:"his_dynasty",cat:"his",t:"Dynasty",
 s:"A family whose members rule one after another for a long time.",
 l:"A dynasty is a line of rulers from the same family, with power usually passing from parent to child or to another relative. Chinese history is traditionally divided into dynasties such as the Han, Tang and Ming. Europe had the Habsburgs, the Bourbons and, in Britain, the Tudors and Stuarts. The word is also used more loosely for any family that stays powerful for generations, for example in business or sport.",
 x:"The Ming dynasty ruled China for nearly three hundred years and built much of the Great Wall we see today.",
 f:"Japan's imperial family is traditionally regarded as the oldest continuous hereditary monarchy in the world.",
 js:"A family where the rulers come one after another.",
 jl:"When a king or queen's son or daughter becomes the next ruler, and then their child, and so on, that family is a dynasty. Some dynasties ruled for hundreds of years.",
 jx:"In the castle we saw paintings of a whole dynasty – grandfather, father and son, all kings."},
{id:"his_empire",cat:"his",t:"Empire",
 s:"A large state that rules many lands and peoples, usually under one emperor or central power.",
 l:"An empire is a state that controls a wide territory with many different peoples, often gained by conquest. Famous examples include the Roman, Persian, Mongol, Ottoman and British empires. Empires could build roads, cities and laws over huge areas and helped trade and ideas to spread, but they often ruled people against their will and took their wealth. Most empires eventually broke up into smaller states.",
 x:"The Roman Empire built roads so good that some are still used today.",
 f:"The word comes from Latin imperium, “command” or “power”. In the 1920s the British Empire covered about a quarter of the world's land.",
 js:"A very big country that rules many other lands.",
 jl:"An empire is like a giant country made of many lands. Long ago, the Romans had an empire that stretched across Europe, Africa and Asia. They built roads, bridges and baths everywhere.",
 jx:"We learned that Roman soldiers walked all over their empire on straight stone roads."},
{id:"his_archive",cat:"his",t:"Archive",
 s:"A collection of old documents and records, or the place where they are kept.",
 l:"An archive keeps records that are worth preserving: letters, maps, court files, birth registers, photographs, films and, today, digital files. Unlike a library, which collects published books, an archive mostly holds unique, unpublished materials created by people and institutions. Archivists organise, protect and catalogue them so that historians, journalists and families can use them. Many archives now put scans online, which has made family history research very popular.",
 x:"In the city archive, Ben found the record of his great-great-grandparents' wedding.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek archeion – the office of the town's magistrates, where official documents were kept.",
 js:"A place where old papers and photos are kept safe.",
 jl:"An archive is like a treasure room for papers. It keeps old letters, maps, photos and school records safe for a very long time, so people can study them later.",
 jx:"The school archive still has a photo of my mum's class from 1995."},
{id:"his_guild",cat:"his",t:"Guild",
 s:"A medieval association of craftsmen or merchants in the same trade.",
 l:"In medieval towns, bakers, shoemakers, goldsmiths and many other trades formed guilds. A guild set rules for quality and prices, trained young people and looked after members who were ill or old. Training had steps: an apprentice learned from a master, became a journeyman and could finally become a master. Guilds also had political power in many towns. They protected their members but could block newcomers and competition.",
 x:"In the Middle Ages you could not simply open a bakery – you had to be accepted by the bakers' guild.",
 f:"A “masterpiece” was originally the piece of work a journeyman had to make to prove to the guild that he was good enough to become a master.",
 js:"A club for people who do the same job, from long ago.",
 jl:"Long ago, all the bakers in a town joined a group called a guild. So did the shoemakers and the blacksmiths. The guild taught young helpers, checked that the work was good and helped members who were ill.",
 jx:"On the old house there's a picture of a pretzel – the sign of the bakers' guild."},
{id:"his_chivalry",cat:"his",t:"Chivalry",
 s:"The medieval code of behaviour expected of a good knight.",
 l:"Chivalry was the ideal of how a knight should behave: brave in battle, loyal to his lord, faithful to the Church, generous, and protective of the weak. Poems and romances, such as the stories of King Arthur, made these ideals famous. Real knights did not always live up to them. Today “chivalry” often means old-fashioned politeness, for example holding a door open for someone.",
 x:"In the legends, chivalry required a knight to keep his word even to an enemy.",
 f:"The word comes from French chevalier, “knight” – literally “horseman”, from cheval, “horse”.",
 js:"The rules for how a good knight should behave.",
 jl:"Knights were supposed to be brave, honest and kind, and to protect people who needed help. These rules were called chivalry. In stories, the best knights always kept their promises.",
 jx:"In the school play, Leo was a knight who showed chivalry by helping an old woman cross the river."},
{id:"his_manuscript",cat:"his",t:"Manuscript",
 s:"A book or document written by hand.",
 l:"Before printing, every book was a manuscript, copied letter by letter, often by monks in a writing room called a scriptorium. Copying a large book could take months or years. Many medieval manuscripts were written on parchment made from animal skin and decorated with coloured pictures and gold, called illuminations. Today “manuscript” also means an author's text before it is published.",
 x:"The Book of Kells, an Irish manuscript from around the year 800, is famous for its swirling decorations.",
 f:"Manuscript comes from Latin manu scriptus, “written by hand”. Parchment is named after the ancient city of Pergamon, an important centre for making it.",
 js:"A book written by hand.",
 jl:"Before printing machines, every book was written by hand, often by monks with feather pens. They painted tiny pictures in bright colours and even gold. Such a book is called a manuscript.",
 jx:"In the library we saw a manuscript with a golden dragon around the first letter."},
{id:"his_historiography",cat:"his",t:"Historiography",
 s:"The writing of history, and the study of how history has been written.",
 l:"Historiography looks at how historians work: which sources they choose, what questions they ask and how their own time and views shape their stories. The same event, such as a revolution, may be described very differently by writers from different countries or centuries. Studying historiography helps us see that history is an ongoing conversation based on evidence, not a fixed list of facts.",
 x:"In the historiography class we compared how British and French historians wrote about the Battle of Waterloo.",
 f:"The Greek writer Herodotus, who lived in the 5th century BC, is often called the “father of history” – a title first given to him by the Roman orator Cicero.",
 js:"How people write about history, and how that changes.",
 jl:"Two people can tell the same story in different ways. Historians are like that too. Historiography is about how history books are written and why they don't always agree.",
 jx:"Our teacher showed us two old textbooks that told the same battle completely differently."},
{id:"his_prehistory",cat:"his",t:"Prehistory",
 s:"The time before people kept written records.",
 l:"Prehistory covers the very long period of human life before writing. Because there are no written texts, we learn about it from objects: stone tools, bones, cave paintings, graves and the remains of houses. Archaeologists divide it into ages such as the Stone Age. Writing appeared in Mesopotamia and Egypt about five thousand years ago, but in other parts of the world prehistory ended much later.",
 x:"Stonehenge was built in prehistory, so nobody left a written explanation of why.",
 f:"The famous painted cave of Lascaux in France, with images around seventeen thousand years old, was discovered in 1940 by four teenagers.",
 js:"The time before people could write.",
 jl:"A very, very long time ago, people didn't know how to write. They made tools from stone and painted animals on cave walls. We call this time prehistory. We learn about it from things people left behind.",
 jx:"In the museum we saw stone axes from prehistory."},
{id:"his_bronze_age",cat:"his",t:"Bronze Age",
 s:"The period when people made tools and weapons mainly from bronze.",
 l:"The Bronze Age came after the Stone Age and before the Iron Age. People learned to mix copper with tin to make bronze, a hard metal for tools, weapons and jewellery. Because tin and copper were often found far apart, the Bronze Age encouraged long-distance trade. It began at different times in different places – in the Near East more than five thousand years ago, later in northern Europe. Cities, kingdoms and early writing grew in this period.",
 x:"The archaeologists found a Bronze Age axe buried in the field.",
 f:"The idea of dividing the past into Stone, Bronze and Iron Ages comes from the Danish museum curator Christian Jürgensen Thomsen, who used it to arrange his collection in the 1800s.",
 js:"A time long ago when people made things from bronze.",
 jl:"After the Stone Age, people found out that mixing two metals, copper and tin, makes bronze. Bronze is strong and shiny, so they made knives, swords and jewellery from it. That time is called the Bronze Age.",
 jx:"The shiny bracelet in the glass case is from the Bronze Age."},
{id:"his_iron_curtain",cat:"his",t:"Iron Curtain",
 s:"The name for the border that divided Europe during the Cold War.",
 l:"The Iron Curtain was the dividing line between Western Europe and the communist states of Eastern Europe controlled by the Soviet Union. It was both a symbol and a real, heavily guarded border with fences, watchtowers and minefields in many places. For decades it was very hard for people in the East to travel freely to the West. It fell apart in 1989, when Hungary opened its border and the Berlin Wall came down.",
 x:"My grandmother grew up behind the Iron Curtain and saw Paris for the first time at sixty.",
 f:"Winston Churchill made the phrase famous in a speech in Fulton, Missouri, on 5 March 1946: “From Stettin in the Baltic to Trieste in the Adriatic, an iron curtain has descended across the Continent.” An “iron curtain” was originally a fireproof curtain in a theatre.",
 js:"A border that split Europe into two parts for many years.",
 jl:"For about forty years, Europe was split into two parts, and it was hard to travel from one to the other. People called the border the Iron Curtain, though it was really fences and walls. In 1989 it opened, and families could visit each other again.",
 jx:"Grandpa said that before 1989 the Iron Curtain stopped him visiting his cousin."},
{id:"his_propaganda",cat:"his",t:"Propaganda",
 s:"Information spread on purpose to push people towards a particular opinion, often by distorting the truth.",
 l:"Propaganda is one-sided messaging meant to shape what large groups of people think and do. It uses posters, films, speeches, songs, news and now social media. It often repeats simple slogans, plays on fear or pride, shows enemies as less than human and hides inconvenient facts. Governments used it heavily in the world wars and under dictatorships. Learning to spot it – asking who made a message and why – is an important skill.",
 x:"The wartime poster showing the enemy as monsters was clearly propaganda.",
 f:"The word comes from a Catholic body founded in 1622, the Congregation for Propagating the Faith – at first it simply meant “spreading” a belief, without the negative meaning it has now.",
 js:"Messages made to trick people into thinking one way.",
 jl:"Propaganda is when someone shows only one side of a story, over and over, to make people believe what they want. It can be in posters, films or on the internet. A good question to ask is: who made this, and why?",
 jx:"Our teacher showed us an old poster and asked us to find the tricks propaganda uses."},
{id:"his_french_revolution",cat:"his",t:"French Revolution",
 s:"The uprising that began in France in 1789, ended absolute monarchy and spread ideas of liberty and equality.",
 l:"The French Revolution began in 1789, when anger at royal debts, hunger and the privileges of nobles and clergy exploded. In July Parisians stormed the Bastille prison; in August the National Assembly adopted the Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen. The monarchy was abolished in 1792. The Revolution also turned violent, with the Terror and many executions, and it ended with Napoleon taking power in 1799. Its ideals deeply influenced modern Europe.",
 x:"The French Revolution's motto, “Liberty, equality, fraternity”, is still the motto of France.",
 f:"When the Bastille was stormed on 14 July 1789, it held only seven prisoners. The day is now France's national holiday.",
 js:"When the people of France rose up against their king in 1789.",
 jl:"In 1789 many people in France were poor and hungry, while the king and nobles lived in luxury. The people protested and changed the country. They said all people should be free and equal. This is called the French Revolution.",
 jx:"On 14 July, French towns have fireworks to remember the start of the French Revolution."},
{id:"his_armistice",cat:"his",t:"Armistice",
 s:"An agreement to stop fighting, which usually comes before a peace treaty.",
 l:"An armistice is a formal agreement between warring sides to stop fighting. It is not yet peace: the terms of peace are settled later in a treaty. The best-known armistice ended the fighting of the First World War at eleven o'clock in the morning on 11 November 1918. It was signed in a railway carriage in the forest of Compiègne in France. The Treaty of Versailles followed in 1919.",
 x:"The armistice stopped the guns, but the peace conference took months.",
 f:"Many countries still mark 11 November. In Britain people wear red poppies and keep two minutes' silence; in Poland it is Independence Day.",
 js:"An agreement to stop fighting.",
 jl:"When two sides in a war agree to stop fighting, they sign an armistice. The most famous one ended the First World War on 11 November 1918, at eleven o'clock in the morning.",
 jx:"At school we kept a minute's silence to remember the armistice of 1918."},
{id:"his_en_magna_carta",cat:"his",only:1,c:"England",t:"Magna Carta",
 s:"The charter of 1215 that limited the power of the English king.",
 l:"Magna Carta, Latin for “Great Charter”, was agreed by King John at Runnymede, near Windsor, in June 1215, after rebellious barons forced him to accept limits on his power. It promised, among other things, that no free man could be imprisoned or punished except by lawful judgement or the law of the land. It soon failed as a peace deal, but it was reissued many times and became a symbol of the idea that even rulers are subject to the law.",
 x:"Campaigners for fair trials often quote Magna Carta.",
 f:"Four original copies of the 1215 charter survive. Only three of its clauses are still part of the law of England and Wales.",
 js:"An old paper from 1215 that said even the king must obey the law.",
 jl:"In 1215, King John of England was very unpopular. Powerful lords made him sign a paper called Magna Carta. It said the king could not just throw people in prison without a fair decision. It's one of the most famous papers in history.",
 jx:"In the museum, we saw one of the old copies of Magna Carta behind glass."},
{id:"his_en_domesday",cat:"his",only:1,c:"England",t:"Domesday Book",
 s:"The great survey of England's land and property made for William the Conqueror in 1086.",
 l:"Twenty years after conquering England in 1066, William the Conqueror sent officials around the country to record who held each piece of land, how many people lived there, and how many ploughs, mills and animals there were – mainly so that he knew what taxes he could raise. The results were written into the Domesday Book. It is the most complete record of any European country in that period and lets people look up their town or village as it was nearly a thousand years ago.",
 x:"Our village appears in the Domesday Book with a mill and twelve households.",
 f:"It got its name because, like the Last Judgement – “Doomsday” – its verdicts could not be appealed. It is kept at The National Archives in Kew.",
 js:"A giant list of England's land and people made almost a thousand years ago.",
 jl:"In 1086, King William wanted to know what everyone in England owned. His helpers counted farms, mills, pigs and cows in every village and wrote it all in a huge book, the Domesday Book. You can still look up many villages in it!",
 jx:"We found our town in the Domesday Book – it had only twenty families then."},
{id:"his_en_suffragettes",cat:"his",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Suffragettes",
 s:"Women who campaigned, often with direct action, for women's right to vote in the early 20th century.",
 l:"The suffragettes were members of the Women's Social and Political Union, founded in 1903 by Emmeline Pankhurst and her daughters. Frustrated by peaceful petitions being ignored, they used marches, heckling and later more militant tactics, such as breaking windows. Many were imprisoned. Alongside the peaceful suffragists, they helped win the vote for some women over thirty in 1918 and for women on equal terms with men in 1928.",
 x:"The suffragettes' colours were purple, white and green.",
 f:"The word “suffragette” was first used by the Daily Mail in 1906 as a mocking name – but the women adopted it with pride.",
 js:"Women who fought for women to be allowed to vote.",
 jl:"About a hundred years ago, women in Britain were not allowed to vote. The suffragettes marched, gave speeches and protested loudly to change that. Many were sent to prison, but in the end women won the right to vote.",
 jx:"In the history project, Amira dressed up as a suffragette with a purple, white and green sash."},
{id:"his_en_tudors",cat:"his",only:1,c:"England",t:"The Tudors",
 s:"The royal family that ruled England from 1485 to 1603.",
 l:"The Tudor dynasty began when Henry Tudor won the Battle of Bosworth in 1485 and became Henry VII, ending the Wars of the Roses. His son Henry VIII broke with the Pope and founded the Church of England. The last Tudor, Elizabeth I, ruled for forty-four years, a time of exploration, the defeat of the Spanish Armada in 1588 and the theatre of Shakespeare. The dynasty ended when Elizabeth died without children in 1603.",
 x:"Black-and-white timber-framed houses are often called Tudor style.",
 f:"The Tudor rose combines the red rose of the House of Lancaster and the white rose of the House of York, the two families of the Wars of the Roses.",
 js:"A famous family of kings and queens in England long ago.",
 jl:"The Tudors were kings and queens of England about five hundred years ago. The most famous are Henry VIII, who had six wives, and his daughter Elizabeth I, who ruled for a long time while Shakespeare was writing his plays.",
 jx:"In class we made Tudor ruffs out of paper plates."},
/* ---------- MEDICINE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"m_acute_chronic",cat:"med",t:"Acute and chronic",
 s:"Acute means sudden and usually short; chronic means long-lasting.",
 l:"Doctors describe illnesses by how they develop over time. An acute condition starts suddenly and lasts a short time, like a cold, a sprained ankle or appendicitis – though acute can also be serious. A chronic condition lasts a long time or keeps coming back, often defined as more than three months, like asthma, diabetes or long-term back pain. Chronic conditions are usually managed rather than cured in one go, so living well with them is a big part of care.",
 x:"Her acute pain after the fall went away in a week, but her father's chronic back pain needs regular physiotherapy.",
 f:"Acute comes from Latin acutus, “sharp”; chronic comes from Greek chronos, “time”.",
 js:"Acute means it starts suddenly and goes away; chronic means it lasts a long time.",
 jl:"Some illnesses come quickly and soon go away, like a cold – doctors call them acute. Others stay for a long time, like asthma – those are chronic. People with chronic illnesses learn how to take care of themselves every day.",
 jx:"Ollie's stomach ache was acute – it was gone by the next morning."},
{id:"m_contraindication",cat:"med",t:"Contraindication",
 s:"A reason why a particular treatment should not be used for a particular person.",
 l:"A contraindication is a situation in which a medicine, procedure or activity may be harmful, so it should be avoided or used only with great care. It can be an illness, an allergy, pregnancy, age or another medicine taken at the same time. The opposite, an indication, is the reason a treatment is recommended. Contraindications are listed in the leaflet that comes with every medicine, which is why doctors and pharmacists ask about your health before prescribing.",
 x:"The pharmacist said that a stomach ulcer is a contraindication for some common painkillers.",
 f:"The word joins Latin contra, “against”, with indicare, “to point out” – something that points against using a treatment.",
 js:"A reason not to use a certain medicine.",
 jl:"Some medicines are good for most people but not for everyone. If something about your body means a medicine could hurt you, like an allergy, that's called a contraindication. That's why the doctor asks lots of questions first.",
 jx:"Because Sara is allergic to penicillin, the doctor chose a different medicine for her."},
{id:"m_symptom_sign",cat:"med",t:"Symptom and sign",
 s:"A symptom is what the patient feels; a sign is what someone else can observe or measure.",
 l:"In medicine, symptoms are subjective: pain, tiredness, nausea or dizziness are felt by the patient and can only be described. Signs are objective: a rash, a fast heartbeat, a high temperature on a thermometer or swelling can be seen or measured by a doctor. Some things can be both, such as coughing. Together, symptoms and signs help the doctor build a picture of what is wrong, often before any test.",
 x:"Her headache was a symptom; the stiff neck the doctor noticed during the examination was a sign.",
 f:"Symptom comes from Greek sýmptōma, “something that happens” or “a chance occurrence”.",
 js:"A symptom is what you feel; a sign is what the doctor can see.",
 jl:"When you're ill, you might feel a headache – that's a symptom, because only you can feel it. The doctor might see red spots on your skin – that's a sign, because others can see it too. Both help the doctor find out what's wrong.",
 jx:"Ben said his throat hurt – a symptom. The doctor saw his throat was red – a sign."},
{id:"m_vaccine",cat:"med",t:"Vaccine",
 s:"A preparation that trains the immune system to recognise a germ before it causes illness.",
 l:"A vaccine shows the immune system a harmless version or part of a germ, or instructions to make such a part, so that the body learns to recognise it. If the real germ arrives later, the body can react quickly, often preventing illness or making it much milder. Vaccines have greatly reduced diseases such as measles, polio and tetanus, and smallpox was eradicated worldwide. When many people are vaccinated, they also help protect those who cannot be.",
 x:"Before her trip, the nurse checked which vaccines she needed.",
 f:"The word comes from Latin vacca, “cow”. In 1796 Edward Jenner showed that infection with mild cowpox protected people against deadly smallpox.",
 js:"A medicine that teaches your body to fight germs before you get ill.",
 jl:"A vaccine is like a practice drill for your body. It shows your body what a germ looks like, without making you really ill. Then, if the real germ comes, your body already knows how to fight it.",
 jx:"Lily was brave at the nurse's office – her vaccine was over in a second."},
{id:"m_generic",cat:"med",t:"Generic medicine",
 s:"A medicine with the same active substance as a brand-name medicine, usually cheaper.",
 l:"When a company invents a medicine, a patent protects it for a number of years. After the patent ends, other companies can make generic versions with the same active substance, strength and form. Before approval they must show that the generic works in the body in the same way, which is called bioequivalence. The packaging, colour or filler ingredients may differ. Generics are usually much cheaper, which helps patients and health systems.",
 x:"The pharmacist offered a generic medicine with the same active ingredient at half the price.",
 f:"Most medicines have an international common name chosen through the World Health Organization, so the same substance can be recognised worldwide whatever brand name it has.",
 js:"A cheaper copy of a medicine that works the same way.",
 jl:"When a new medicine is invented, only one company can sell it for some years. After that, other companies can make the same medicine. These copies are called generics. They work the same but usually cost less.",
 jx:"Mum's tablets looked different this month, but the pharmacist said it's the same generic medicine."},
{id:"m_inflammation",cat:"med",t:"Inflammation",
 s:"The body's response to injury or infection, with redness, heat, swelling and pain.",
 l:"Inflammation is the body's way of defending and repairing itself. When tissue is hurt or germs enter, blood vessels widen and let more blood and immune cells reach the area, which causes redness, warmth and swelling, and nerves signal pain. Short-term inflammation is helpful and fades as the body heals. Long-lasting, chronic inflammation, however, can damage tissue and is part of conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis.",
 x:"The splinter caused a small inflammation – the skin around it was red and warm.",
 f:"The Roman writer Celsus described four classic signs of inflammation in Latin: rubor, calor, tumor and dolor – redness, heat, swelling and pain. A fifth, loss of function, was added later.",
 js:"When part of your body gets red, warm and swollen to heal itself.",
 jl:"If you cut your knee, it may get red, warm and a bit puffy. That's inflammation. It's your body sending helpers to the spot to fight germs and fix the skin.",
 jx:"After the bee sting, Max's hand had some inflammation, so we put a cold pack on it."},
/* ---------- LAW (+6, England & Wales) ---------- */
{id:"l_presumption",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Presumption of innocence",
 s:"The rule that a person accused of a crime is treated as innocent until proven guilty.",
 l:"In England and Wales, the burden of proof in a criminal trial lies on the prosecution: it is not for the defendant to prove they are innocent. The jury or magistrates may convict only if they are sure of guilt – the traditional phrase is “beyond reasonable doubt”. The principle is also protected by Article 6 of the European Convention on Human Rights, part of UK law through the Human Rights Act 1998. Media coverage should respect it too.",
 x:"Because of the presumption of innocence, the defendant did not have to prove where he was that night.",
 f:"In the 1935 case Woolmington v DPP, a judge called the prosecution's duty to prove guilt the “golden thread” running through English criminal law.",
 js:"Everyone is treated as innocent until it is proved they did something wrong.",
 jl:"If someone is accused of doing something bad, the court must first prove it. Until then, the person is treated as innocent. It's fair: imagine being blamed for a broken window you didn't break!",
 jx:"The teacher didn't punish anyone until she knew who had spilled the paint – that's like the presumption of innocence."},
{id:"l_limitation",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Limitation period",
 s:"The time limit for starting a court claim.",
 l:"In England and Wales, the Limitation Act 1980 sets deadlines for bringing civil claims. In general, claims for breach of contract and most torts must be started within six years, and personal injury claims within three years, usually counted from the injury or from when the person first knew about it. After the deadline the other side can use it as a defence. Time limits exist so that cases are decided while evidence is fresh and people are not threatened with old claims forever.",
 x:"The solicitor warned him that the limitation period for his claim would run out next spring.",
 f:"Serious crimes in England and Wales generally have no time limit for prosecution, while the least serious offences usually must be charged within six months.",
 js:"A time limit for asking a court for help.",
 jl:"If someone owes you something, you can ask a court to help – but not forever. There is a deadline. After many years, it's too late. This deadline is called a limitation period.",
 jx:"Dad said if you want to complain, don't wait for years – there's a time limit."},
{id:"l_power_attorney",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Lasting power of attorney",
 s:"A legal document that lets you choose someone to make decisions for you if you lose the ability to.",
 l:"In England and Wales, a lasting power of attorney (LPA) lets an adult choose one or more trusted people, called attorneys, to act for them. There are two types: one for property and financial affairs, and one for health and welfare. It must be registered with the Office of the Public Guardian before it can be used. Making one while you are well means your chosen people, not a court, can step in if illness or an accident affects your ability to decide.",
 x:"After her diagnosis, Gran made a lasting power of attorney so my mum could manage her bank account if needed.",
 f:"An “attorney” here does not have to be a lawyer – it can be any trusted adult, such as a relative or friend.",
 js:"A paper that lets someone you trust make choices for you.",
 jl:"Sometimes grown-ups write a special paper saying: “If I can't decide things myself, I want this person to help.” It's called a power of attorney. It makes sure someone they trust looks after them.",
 jx:"Grandpa chose Aunt Kate to help with his money if he ever needs it."},
{id:"l_appeal",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Appeal",
 s:"Asking a higher court to review and change a lower court's decision.",
 l:"An appeal is a request to a higher court to look again at a decision, usually because a judge made a legal mistake or the process was unfair. In England and Wales, appeals can go from the lower courts to the High Court or the Court of Appeal, and on important legal points to the Supreme Court, which replaced the House of Lords as the top court in 2009. An appeal is not usually a whole new trial, and permission to appeal is often needed.",
 x:"The company lost the case but announced it would appeal.",
 f:"Until 2009, the highest court in the United Kingdom was a committee of the House of Lords, part of Parliament.",
 js:"Asking a higher court to check if a decision was fair.",
 jl:"If someone thinks a judge made a wrong decision, they can ask a higher court to check it. That's an appeal. It's a bit like asking the head teacher to look again at a decision.",
 jx:"The football club appealed against the red card, and the higher committee agreed."},
{id:"l_contract",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Contract",
 s:"A legally binding agreement between two or more parties.",
 l:"In English law, a contract usually needs an offer, its acceptance, something of value exchanged by each side – called consideration – and an intention to be legally bound. Most contracts can be made orally or even by conduct, such as paying at a till; writing simply makes them easier to prove. Some must be in writing, for example a contract to sell land. If one side breaks the contract, the other can usually claim damages.",
 x:"When you buy a coffee, you make a small contract: the café gives you coffee and you pay for it.",
 f:"A promise of a gift, with nothing given in return, is generally not a binding contract in English law unless it is made by a formal deed.",
 js:"An agreement that the law makes people keep.",
 jl:"A contract is an agreement: you give something and get something back. When you buy an ice cream, you pay money and get the ice cream – that's a tiny contract! Some contracts are written on paper and signed.",
 jx:"Before the guitar lessons began, Mum signed a contract with the music school."},
{id:"l_en_magistrates",cat:"law",only:1,c:"England & Wales",t:"Magistrates' court",
 s:"The court where almost all criminal cases in England and Wales begin, often run by volunteer judges.",
 l:"Every criminal case in England and Wales starts in a magistrates' court, and most are completed there. Magistrates, also called justices of the peace, are usually unpaid volunteers from the local community who do not need legal qualifications. They typically sit in a panel of three, advised on the law by a legal adviser. Some courts have a professional district judge instead. More serious cases are sent to the Crown Court, where a jury decides.",
 x:"The speeding case was dealt with at the local magistrates' court in one morning.",
 f:"Justices of the peace have existed in England for centuries; an important law about them, the Justices of the Peace Act, dates from 1361.",
 js:"A local court where ordinary volunteers help decide cases.",
 jl:"In England, many small cases are decided in a magistrates' court. The people who decide are often volunteers from the town, not professional judges. They listen carefully and get advice about the law.",
 jx:"Our neighbour is a magistrate – she helps at the court one day a week."},
/* ---------- EVERYDAY LANGUAGE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"g_euphemism",cat:"gen",t:"Euphemism",
 s:"A gentler word or phrase used instead of one that might seem harsh or embarrassing.",
 l:"A euphemism softens a difficult topic. People say “passed away” instead of “died”, “let go” instead of “fired”, or “restroom” instead of “toilet”. Euphemisms can be kind and tactful, but they can also hide the truth, for example when “collateral damage” is used for civilian deaths in war. For many neurodivergent people, euphemisms can be confusing, so plain words are sometimes the kindest choice.",
 x:"The manager used the euphemism “rightsizing” when he meant that people were losing their jobs.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek eu, “good”, and phēmē, “speech” – literally “speaking well”.",
 js:"A softer way of saying something unpleasant.",
 jl:"Sometimes people use nicer words for things that feel awkward. Saying “I need to use the bathroom” instead of “toilet” is an example. These gentle words are called euphemisms.",
 jx:"When Grandpa says the cake is “interesting”, it's a euphemism – he doesn't like it."},
{id:"g_irony",cat:"gen",t:"Irony",
 s:"Saying the opposite of what you mean, or a situation that turns out the opposite of what was expected.",
 l:"Verbal irony means saying one thing while meaning another, often the opposite – like saying “Lovely weather!” in a storm. Situational irony is when events turn out the reverse of what you would expect, such as a fire station burning down. Irony depends on tone, context and shared knowledge, so it is easy to miss in text messages, and many autistic people prefer clear statements. Sarcasm is a sharper, often unkind form of verbal irony.",
 x:"“Great, another Monday,” she said with irony as the alarm went off.",
 f:"It comes from Greek eirōneía, “pretended ignorance”. The philosopher Socrates was famous for pretending to know nothing so that others would reveal the gaps in their reasoning – called Socratic irony.",
 js:"Saying the opposite of what you mean, as a joke.",
 jl:"If it's pouring with rain and someone says “What a sunny day!”, they don't really mean it. That's irony. People use a special voice for it, but it can be hard to notice – it's always okay to ask, “Do you mean that?”",
 jx:"When the bus finally came after an hour, Dad said, “Right on time!” – that was irony."},
{id:"g_idiom",cat:"gen",t:"Idiom",
 s:"A fixed phrase whose meaning cannot be guessed from the meanings of its individual words.",
 l:"An idiom is an expression like “it's raining cats and dogs” or “break a leg”, whose meaning is different from what the words say. Idioms are learned as whole units and are often hard for language learners, because translating them word for word produces nonsense. Every language has its own, and some express the same idea with completely different images. Knowing idioms makes speech sound natural; explaining them helps people who take words literally.",
 x:"When Jake said the test was “a piece of cake”, his German friend asked where the cake was.",
 f:"When English speakers are exhausted they are “dead on their feet”; Polish speakers say they are “falling off their legs” – the same feeling, a different picture.",
 js:"A saying that means something different from its words.",
 jl:"When someone says “I'm all ears”, they don't really turn into ears – it means they are listening carefully. A phrase like this is an idiom. They're fun to learn, like secret codes of a language.",
 jx:"“Hold your horses!” said Mum, which meant: wait a moment."},
{id:"g_paradox",cat:"gen",t:"Paradox",
 s:"A statement or situation that seems to contradict itself but may still be true.",
 l:"A paradox seems impossible or self-contradictory at first, but on closer thought may reveal something true or interesting. “Less is more” is a simple one. Some paradoxes are logical puzzles, like “This sentence is false” – if it's true, it's false, and if it's false, it's true. Others describe real life, such as the paradox of choice: having more options can make it harder to decide and to feel satisfied.",
 x:"It's a paradox that the more I try to fall asleep, the more awake I feel.",
 f:"The Greek thinker Zeno of Elea created famous paradoxes, such as the claim that the fast runner Achilles could never overtake a tortoise with a head start.",
 js:"Something that sounds impossible but can be true.",
 jl:"A paradox is a puzzle that seems to say two opposite things at once. For example: “I always lie.” If that's true, then it's a lie! Paradoxes make your brain do somersaults.",
 jx:"“The only rule is that there are no rules” – Ana said that's a paradox."},
{id:"g_oxymoron",cat:"gen",t:"Oxymoron",
 s:"A phrase that puts two contradictory words side by side.",
 l:"An oxymoron joins words with opposite meanings to create a striking effect: “deafening silence”, “bittersweet”, “living dead” or “organised chaos”. Poets use oxymorons to express mixed feelings – Shakespeare's Romeo speaks of “sweet sorrow” and “cold fire”. Some everyday phrases, like “jumbo shrimp” or “seriously funny”, are often joked about as accidental oxymorons. It differs from a paradox, which is a whole statement or idea, not just a pair of words.",
 x:"The silence after the bad news was deafening – a perfect oxymoron.",
 f:"The word itself is an oxymoron: in Greek, oxys means “sharp” and mōros means “dull” or “foolish” – so it means “sharp-dull”.",
 js:"Two words with opposite meanings put together.",
 jl:"An oxymoron is when you put two opposite words together, like “bittersweet” or “awfully good”. It sounds strange, but it can describe mixed feelings really well.",
 jx:"Sam called his messy but tidy-looking desk “organised chaos” – that's an oxymoron."},
{id:"g_en_cheers",cat:"gen",only:1,t:"Cheers",
 s:"In British English, a word used to say thank you, goodbye, or as a toast.",
 l:"“Cheers!” is one of the most flexible words in British English. Raising glasses, it's a toast. Handed a coffee or held for at a door, it's a casual “thank you”. At the end of a phone call or an email, it can mean “bye” or “best wishes”. It sounds friendly and informal, so it's common among friends and colleagues but less suitable in very formal letters. Americans mainly use it as a toast.",
 x:"“Here's your change.” – “Cheers, mate!”",
 f:"The word once meant “face” or “expression” – from Old French chiere – so being of good cheer meant having a happy face.",
 js:"A friendly British word for “thanks” or “bye”.",
 jl:"In Britain, people say “Cheers!” in lots of ways: to say thank you, to say goodbye, or when they clink glasses at a party. It's a happy, friendly word.",
 jx:"When the bus driver gave Leo his ticket, Leo said, “Cheers!”"},
/* ---------- PSYCHOLOGY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"p_resilience",cat:"psy",t:"Resilience",
 s:"The ability to adapt and recover after difficulties.",
 l:"Resilience is the capacity to cope with stress, setbacks or hard experiences and to adapt over time. It is not about never feeling pain or about being “tough”; resilient people still feel sad, scared or angry. Resilience grows from things like supportive relationships, rest, a sense of meaning and skills for solving problems, and it depends a lot on circumstances, not just on personality. Asking for help is part of it, not a sign of its absence.",
 x:"After failing the exam, she showed resilience: she rested for a few days, asked for tutoring and tried again.",
 f:"The word comes from Latin resilire, “to jump back” or “to rebound” – like a ball that springs back into shape.",
 js:"Being able to get back up after something hard.",
 jl:"Everyone falls sometimes, like when you lose a game or a friend is unkind. Resilience means you can feel sad for a while and then slowly feel better again, especially with help from people who care about you.",
 jx:"Even though Noah lost the race, he showed resilience and signed up for the next one."},
{id:"p_masking",cat:"psy",t:"Masking",
 s:"Hiding or suppressing one's natural behaviour to fit in, often by autistic or ADHD people.",
 l:"Masking, also called camouflaging, means consciously or automatically hiding traits that might be judged, for example by forcing eye contact, copying others' gestures, rehearsing conversations or holding back stimming. Many autistic people and people with ADHD mask to avoid rejection or bullying. It can help in the short term, but research links long-term masking with exhaustion, anxiety and burnout. Accepting environments make masking less necessary.",
 x:"After a whole day of masking at work, he needed a quiet evening alone to recover.",
 f:"Because masking can hide traits so well, it is thought to be one reason why autism in girls and women has often been recognised later than in boys.",
 js:"Hiding the way you really are to fit in with others.",
 jl:"Some people pretend to be different from how they really feel, so others won't think they're strange. That's called masking. It's very tiring, like wearing a heavy costume all day. With good friends, you can take the mask off.",
 jx:"Ava felt she could stop masking when she found friends who liked her just as she is."},
{id:"p_sensory_overload",cat:"psy",t:"Sensory overload",
 s:"When more sound, light, touch or other input arrives than the brain can process comfortably.",
 l:"Sensory overload happens when the senses receive too much at once – loud music, bright lights, crowds, strong smells, scratchy clothes – and the brain cannot filter it. It can cause stress, irritability, confusion, a strong urge to escape, or a shutdown or meltdown. Anyone can experience it, but it is especially common for autistic people and those with ADHD or PTSD. Noise-cancelling headphones, sunglasses, breaks in a quiet space and planning ahead can help.",
 x:"At the busy station she felt sensory overload and put on her headphones until the train came.",
 f:"Some shops and cinemas now offer quiet hours or relaxed screenings, with softer lighting, lower sound and no announcements.",
 js:"When there's too much noise, light or crowd and it feels too much.",
 jl:"Sometimes everything is too loud, too bright or too busy, and your brain feels like a cup that's overflowing. That's sensory overload. Covering your ears, going to a quiet corner or wearing headphones can really help.",
 jx:"At the party, Kai got sensory overload, so he sat in the quiet room with his headphones for a while."},
{id:"p_mindfulness",cat:"psy",t:"Mindfulness",
 s:"Paying attention to the present moment on purpose, without judging it.",
 l:"Mindfulness means noticing what is happening right now – your breath, sounds, body sensations, thoughts and feelings – with curiosity rather than criticism. It can be practised in formal meditation or during everyday activities, like eating or walking. Programmes based on mindfulness are used to help with stress and to prevent depression from returning. It is not about emptying the mind, and it does not suit everyone: some people, for example after trauma, may prefer gentle, guided or movement-based versions.",
 x:"During her mindful walk, she noticed the crunch of leaves and the cold air on her face.",
 f:"In 1979 Jon Kabat-Zinn founded a stress-reduction clinic at the University of Massachusetts, bringing mindfulness practices into Western medicine.",
 js:"Noticing what's happening right now, calmly.",
 jl:"Mindfulness is like being a gentle detective of the present moment. You notice your breathing, the sounds around you and how your body feels, without saying “good” or “bad”. It can help you feel calmer.",
 jx:"Before the test, our class did one minute of mindfulness, listening to our breathing."},
{id:"p_dunning_kruger",cat:"psy",t:"Dunning–Kruger effect",
 s:"The tendency of people with little skill in an area to overestimate their ability.",
 l:"The Dunning–Kruger effect describes a pattern found in studies: people who perform poorly at a task often rate themselves as above average, partly because the knowledge needed to do something well is also needed to notice one's mistakes. Experts, in contrast, sometimes slightly underestimate themselves. Some researchers argue the effect is partly a statistical artefact, and it is often exaggerated online. Its useful lesson is that everyone can be overconfident in areas they don't know well.",
 x:"After one video, he thought he could fix the car himself – a textbook case of the Dunning–Kruger effect.",
 f:"It is named after psychologists David Dunning and Justin Kruger, whose 1999 paper was titled “Unskilled and Unaware of It”.",
 js:"When people who know only a little think they know a lot.",
 jl:"Have you ever thought something was easy, then found out it was much harder? When we know only a little about something, we sometimes think we're experts. Scientists call this the Dunning–Kruger effect.",
 jx:"After one chess lesson, Theo said he could beat anyone – then lost in five moves."},
{id:"p_spotlight",cat:"psy",t:"Spotlight effect",
 s:"The tendency to think that other people notice our appearance and mistakes far more than they do.",
 l:"The spotlight effect is the feeling that everyone is watching and judging us – the stain on our shirt, the word we stumbled on, the awkward moment. In reality most people are busy thinking about themselves. Knowing about this effect can ease social anxiety: the mistake that feels huge to you is often barely noticed, and quickly forgotten, by others.",
 x:"She worried all day about her messy hair, but thanks to the spotlight effect she overestimated how many people noticed.",
 f:"In a well-known study from 2000, students who wore an embarrassing T-shirt guessed that about half of the people in the room noticed it; in fact only about a quarter did.",
 js:"Thinking everyone is looking at you when they really aren't.",
 jl:"When you trip or say something silly, it can feel like everyone saw it. But most people are thinking about themselves and hardly noticed! This feeling is called the spotlight effect.",
 jx:"Zara thought the whole class saw her drop her pencil case, but nobody even remembered."},
/* ---------- BUSINESS (+6) ---------- */
{id:"b_brainstorming",cat:"biz",t:"Brainstorming",
 s:"A group method for producing many ideas quickly, without judging them at first.",
 l:"Brainstorming means collecting as many ideas as possible on a problem before evaluating them. The classic rules are: aim for quantity, don't criticise during the session, welcome unusual ideas and build on others' suggestions. Afterwards the group sorts and judges the ideas. Studies show that people working alone first often produce more ideas, so many teams combine quiet individual thinking with group discussion – a format that can also suit introverts and neurodivergent people.",
 x:"We spent twenty minutes brainstorming names for the new app before choosing three to test.",
 f:"The method was popularised by the American advertising executive Alex Osborn, especially in his 1953 book Applied Imagination.",
 js:"When a group thinks of lots of ideas together.",
 jl:"In brainstorming, everyone shares ideas – even silly ones – and nobody says “That's bad”. You write them all down. Later you choose the best ones. Lots of great ideas start as silly ones!",
 jx:"Our class brainstormed ideas for the school fair: a cake stall, a quiz and a treasure hunt."},
{id:"b_kpi",cat:"biz",t:"KPI (key performance indicator)",
 s:"A number used to measure progress towards an important goal.",
 l:"A key performance indicator, or KPI, is a measurable value that shows how well a company, team or person is achieving a key objective. Examples are monthly sales, customer satisfaction scores, delivery times or the number of app users. Good KPIs are few, clearly defined and linked to real goals. If people focus only on the number, though, they may improve it in ways that miss the actual goal.",
 x:"Our main KPI this quarter is how many customers renew their subscription.",
 f:"A well-known warning, called Goodhart's law after the economist Charles Goodhart, is often summed up as: when a measure becomes a target, it stops being a good measure.",
 js:"A number that shows if you're reaching your goal.",
 jl:"If your goal is to read more, you could count books each month. That number helps you see your progress. Companies do the same with special numbers called KPIs.",
 jx:"Our class reading KPI: thirty books by the end of term."},
{id:"b_stakeholder",cat:"biz",t:"Stakeholder",
 s:"Anyone who affects or is affected by a project or organisation.",
 l:"Stakeholders are all the people and groups with an interest in what an organisation does: owners and investors, employees, customers, suppliers, local residents, government and others. Some have a lot of influence, others are strongly affected but have little say. Good project managers identify stakeholders early, find out what they need and keep them informed, because ignoring an important group can cause a project to fail.",
 x:"Before building the new car park, the council met with stakeholders, including shop owners and neighbours.",
 f:"A stakeholder originally meant the person who held the money, or stakes, of people making a bet until the result was known.",
 js:"Everyone who cares about or is affected by a plan.",
 jl:"If your school plans a new playground, many people care: pupils, teachers, parents, even neighbours. All of them are stakeholders, and it's good to ask what they think.",
 jx:"For the class trip, the stakeholders were pupils, teachers and parents."},
{id:"b_invoice",cat:"biz",t:"Invoice",
 s:"A document asking a customer to pay for goods or services provided.",
 l:"An invoice lists what was sold or done, the price, any tax, the total, the date and when payment is due. It includes details of the seller and the buyer and a unique invoice number. Invoices are important for accounting and taxes, and businesses must usually keep them for several years. An invoice is different from a receipt, which confirms that payment has already been made.",
 x:"The web designer sent her invoice the day after the website went live, with payment due in thirty days.",
 f:"In many countries, invoices between businesses are increasingly sent electronically, in formats that accounting software can read automatically.",
 js:"A paper that says how much you have to pay.",
 jl:"When someone does a job, like fixing a roof, they give a paper that says what they did and how much it costs. That's an invoice. It's like a bill in a restaurant.",
 jx:"The plumber left an invoice on the kitchen table."},
{id:"b_swot",cat:"biz",t:"SWOT analysis",
 s:"A planning tool that lists Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities and Threats.",
 l:"SWOT analysis helps a business, team or even a person look at their situation clearly. Strengths and weaknesses are internal – things you have or lack, such as skills or money. Opportunities and threats are external – trends, competitors, laws or changes in the market. Writing them in four boxes helps you plan: use strengths to grab opportunities and protect weak points from threats. It works best with honest, specific points rather than vague lists.",
 x:"Before opening the café, they did a SWOT analysis: great location, little experience, a new office nearby, and two competitors on the same street.",
 f:"Many people use SWOT for personal decisions too, like choosing a course or a job.",
 js:"Four boxes to think about what's good, what's hard, chances and dangers.",
 jl:"Draw four boxes. Write what you're good at, what's hard for you, good chances around you and things that might go wrong. That's a SWOT analysis – it helps you make a plan.",
 jx:"For our lemonade stand, our SWOT said: tasty lemonade, small table, hot weather, rain."},
{id:"b_en_bluesky",cat:"biz",only:1,t:"Blue-sky thinking",
 s:"Creative thinking that ignores current limits and practical concerns.",
 l:"Blue-sky thinking means imagining ideas freely, as if there were no limits of budget, rules or existing habits – like looking at an open, cloudless sky. It is common in British office talk, especially in planning meetings. Used well, it helps teams escape routine. Because it is a frequent piece of business jargon, people also use it ironically, and it regularly appears on lists of the most annoying office phrases.",
 x:"Let's start with some blue-sky thinking, then see which ideas we can actually afford.",
 f:"British office jargon is famous for sky and weather images: as well as blue-sky thinking, people talk about ideas that are “up in the air” or about a “perfect storm” of problems.",
 js:"Thinking of big ideas without any limits.",
 jl:"Blue-sky thinking means imagining anything at all – as if the sky were clear and endless. What if school was in a treehouse? What if bikes could fly? Some crazy ideas lead to real inventions.",
 jx:"Our teacher said: “Blue-sky thinking time – design your dream classroom!”"}
]);
